  "Question": "Ray’s car averages \\(40\\) miles per gallon of gasoline, and Tom’s car averages \\(10\\) miles per gallon of gasoline. Ray and Tom each drive the same number of miles. What is the cars’ combined rate of miles per gallon of gasoline?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10\\)",
    "B": "\\(16\\)",
    "C": "\\(25\\)",
    "D": "\\(30\\)",
    "E": "\\(40\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The combined rate is total miles driven divided by total gallons used. We may choose a \\(40\\)-mile trip for each car: changing the common trip length scales both total miles and total fuel by the same factor, leaving their ratio unchanged.\n\nRay uses \\(40/40=1\\) gallon, and Tom uses \\(40/10=4\\) gallons. Together they travel \\(80\\) miles using \\(5\\) gallons, so their combined rate is\n\\[\n\\frac{40+40}{1+4}=16\n\\]\nmiles per gallon, choice B.",
